•	The candidate should have 5 years of related IT working experience;
•	Possessing project management certification such as PMP, CITPM or equivalent will be an added advantage;
•	Possess good interpersonal, spoken and written communication skills.

As part of the shortlisting process for this role, you may be required to complete a medical declaration and/or undergo further assessment.

All applicants will be updated on the status of their applications within 4 weeks upon closing of the advertisement. #LI-FL1

jobResponsibilities:
The NS Policy and Recognition (NSPR) branch under the National Service Directorate (NSD) is responsible for the development and review of policies that enable the Home Team to deploy and utilise National Service resources better, as well as recognise the contributions of Home Team National Servicemen. The job holder will be tasked to:
(a) drive and coordinate preparations for NS60 (milestone year to commemorate National Service);
(b) undertake other policy reviews which may be assigned;
(c) work with Home Team Departments (i.e. SPF and SCDF) and MINDEF on preparations for NS60 which is taking place in 2027, as well as see to the implementation of specific initiatives; and
(d) lead or support other policy reviews and initiatives undertaken by the branch.

Your portfolio will be focused on Total Rewards for the Government ICT&SS sector. Working closely with and through agencies with a sizeable tech workforce, you will ensure that our centrally governed frameworks, policies and practices for the tech workforce-at-large remain current, relevant and market competitive.  In particular, you will be responsible for the following key areas:
•	Developing and reviewing centrally managed compensation levers for the Government ICT&SS sector, including the design and oversight of compensation mechanisms as well as competency standards to guide agencies’ hiring decisions and uplifting of competency standards;
•	Oversight of salary benchmarking for Government ICT&SS sector to ensure our tech workforce’s remuneration remains competitive.

